    "insert": "The goal of the research is to reduce teacher workload by employing LLMs models to generate personalized learning materials. Using qualitative reviews and quantitative analyses, the research evaluates the effectiveness of using student information to generate personalized learning materials with LLMs. \n\nYour exposé has a very good motivation and the explanations of different approaches for applying LLMs in the educational field is very informative. The reasons to employ LLMs to generate customized and personalized learning materials is very persuasive. You have a very detailed execution plan for your research to answer the main research question, with very strong consideration of the sub-research questions.\nIn general, the main focus of the research (common thread) is very well and consistently presented throughout the entire exposé. However, there are still some potential improvements I'd like to point out: \n\nFirstly, paragraphs are a great way to organize similar sentences of a bigger context to maintain reader's engagement. The paragraphs in your motivation section are very well written and divided. However, in the approach section, there are many paragraphs with similar context, which can be merged into one to avoid repetition. I suggest you group these wisely, so that there is one main context throughout the text, with explanations, reasons, or sentences supporting or extending the main context. Additionally, you should also use more bridge sentences or connecting words to connect sentences in the same paragraph, to go the the next point smoothly.\n\nSecondly, short and compact sentences are the great tool to deliver your intention clearly. Its readability also benefits readers processing their thought or to perceive the new information. Some sentences in your research make very good points, some are however either very complicated, or contain too many information, especially in the approach section. Thus, I advise you to shorten these sentences, using clauses to extend them, or to split them into different sentences. Moreover, using colloquial terms instead of formal language, along with the occasional unsuitable word choice makes the sentence lose its impact. I recommend proof reading to prevent linguistic errors.\n\nFurthermore, explanations of conclusions and decisions are useful to help the reader understand the importance and relevance of that decision. Some of your statements would benefit from a more detailed explanation. At one point you write: \"Lawyers are also concerned about the models used directly in the school environment\", here I'm wondering, why the lawyers are concerned and I would like some additional context to fill in these gaps. Another example are the parameters for your interview participants like \"each teacher needs at least three years of job experience in high school\" and \"we select three students with a minimum age of 16 from each teacher\", which seem arbitrary without further explanation. I would prefer having more verification arguments leading to these decisions for clarification.\n\nLastly, LLMs are generally different in types and usages. Verifying at least one concrete model helps narrowing the research context. It would also be an advantage to know the LLM beforehand, so the technical requirements can be prepared for the experiments. To this point I suggest you find a suitable LLM, which compatible with the Human-centred design concept, so that the students' data can be used to generate educational materials suitable for the individual needs."
  }
